.\" Manual page for fm84. View with: man ./doc/fm84.1
.TH FM84 1 "2026-11-09" "fm84 0.46.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fm84 \- a synthwave dual\-pane TUI file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fm84
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ LEFT
.RI [ RIGHT ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fm84
is a two\-panel file manager for the terminal, in the line of Norton
Commander and Midnight Commander.
Each panel lists a directory, with tabs of its own; files are copied and
moved from the active panel to the other one.
It has a built\-in viewer with syntax highlighting, hex and image views, a
built\-in editor, find files by name and content, and directory sizes counted
in the background.
.PP
The top border names the user and host fm84 runs as, reversed for root.
Over each panel is its directory, and the git branch it is on when it is in
a repository, read from the repository's files rather than by running git.
A click on the branch lists the repository's local branches, to switch to
another with Enter or a second click on it; that runs
.BR git (1),
2.23 or newer.
At the end of each name is what git says of it:
.B M
modified,
.B S
staged,
.B ?
untracked,
.B !
ignored,
.B U
in conflict; a directory shows the strongest of what is inside it.
Git is asked on a thread of its own, one at a time for each panel, and F11
turns the marks off.
.PP
Anything that touches the disk is done off the thread that draws the screen,
so a network mount that has stopped answering never freezes the interface;
Esc stops waiting on it.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I LEFT
Directory for the left panel.
.TP
.I RIGHT
Directory for the right panel.
.PP
A directory given takes the place of the tab that would have shown; the
panel's other tabs stay.
Without one, a panel opens where it was when fm84 last quit if
.B Remember directories
is on (F11), and in the current directory otherwise.
A directory that does not exist is reported before anything starts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-choosedir " FILE"
On quitting, write the active panel's directory to
.IR FILE ,
for a shell function to
.B cd
to.
The name is written as it is, with nothing after it.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell\-init " SHELL"
Print
.BR cd84 ,
a shell function that starts fm84 with
.B \-\-choosedir
and then goes to that directory, and exit.
.I SHELL
is
.BR bash ", " zsh ", " fish
or
.BR powershell .
See
.BR "LEAVING THE SHELL WHERE FM84 WAS LEFT" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help, with where the config file is, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the options, for a directory whose name starts with a dash.
.SH ARCHIVES
Enter on a
.IR .zip ,
.IR .7z ,
.IR .tar ,
.IR .tar.gz ,
.IR .tar.zst ,
.I .tar.xz
or
.I .tar.bz2
(and
.IR .tgz ,
.IR .tzst ,
.IR .txz ,
.IR .tbz2 )
opens it in the panel like a directory, and its directories open as any do.
The panel's path runs through it, as
.IR ~/photos.zip/2024 ,
so tabs, history, bookmarks, Ctrl+L and remembered directories work inside
one; Backspace at its top comes back out onto the archive.
.PP
An archive is read-only.
F5 copies out of it, a directory whole, asking about files in the way as any
copy does; times and permissions come out as the archive kept them, and links
as links.
Alt+F9, or Ctrl+F9, extracts the whole of the archive under the cursor, or of
the one the panel is in, into the other panel or wherever its dialog is told.
Shift+F3 compares a file inside an archive with one on the disk or in another
archive, copying each out first.
F3 copies a file out to a scratch directory and shows it there; Enter and
Ctrl+O open it from there with the system's program.
F12 and the lines under the panels read entries in place, and Space and
Alt+Shift+Enter size directories from what the archive says.
Everything that would write into an archive says so and changes nothing:
renaming, making, deleting, moving, copying or linking into it, Alt+A and F4.
Find files does not reach inside one.
.PP
Alt+P packs what is chosen into a new
.IR .zip ,
.IR .7z ,
.IR .tar.gz ,
.IR .tar.xz ,
.I .tar.bz2
or
.IR .tar ,
as a job like a copy, keeping modes, times and links.
A zip or a 7z cannot hold a name that is not UTF-8, which is asked about; a
tar keeps it.
A 7z is packed solid.
The archive is written beside where it goes and put there whole, so a cancel
leaves nothing, and one made inside the directory being packed leaves itself
out.
One already at the name is written over only when the options say to
overwrite.
A
.I .tar.zst
is read but not made.
.PP
A compressed tar is read on past the end of its first stream, so files joined
end to end are one archive.
A 7z is listed from its header without unpacking anything; one packed solid
unpacks what comes before a file to reach it.
.PP
A name that would land outside where it is copied to, with
.BR .. ,
an absolute path or a drive, is left out of the listing, as is anything
that would be written through a link in the archive.
An encrypted zip entry is marked in the detail lines, and cannot be copied
out without the password, which fm84 does not ask for.
Nothing in an encrypted 7z can be copied out either, and one whose names
are encrypted too cannot be gone into.
.PP
An archive written while a panel is in it is read again; one deleted is
left.
The scratch directory is removed when fm84 quits.
.SH LEAVING THE SHELL WHERE FM84 WAS LEFT
A program cannot change the directory of the shell that started it, so
quitting fm84 leaves the shell where it was.
.B cd84
does the rest: it runs fm84, and on quitting goes to the directory of the
active panel, passing on its arguments and its exit status.
It stays put when fm84 did not quit normally, or quit with Shift+F10.
To have it, add one line to the shell's startup file:
.TP
bash, in ~/.bashrc
.B eval \(dq$(fm84 \-\-shell\-init bash)\(dq
.TP
zsh, in ~/.zshrc
.B eval \(dq$(fm84 \-\-shell\-init zsh)\(dq
.TP
fish, in ~/.config/fish/config.fish
.B fm84 \-\-shell\-init fish | source
.TP
PowerShell, in $PROFILE
.B fm84 \-\-shell\-init powershell | Out\-String | Invoke\-Expression
.PP
The function is printed by the fm84 it comes with, so it keeps up with it.
.SH KEYS
F1 inside fm84 lists these too.
.SS Panels
.TP 18
.B Up Down Left Right
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B Tab
Switch panels.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the directory, go into an archive, or open the file with the program
set for its extension in the
.I open
file, or as the system does.
See
.BR ARCHIVES .
.TP
.B Shift+Enter Ctrl+O
Open what is under the cursor as the system does, with the program it opens
that kind of file with; an archive too, which Enter goes into.
Ctrl+O is for terminals that send Shift+Enter as plain Enter; only those with
kitty's keyboard protocol, and Windows, tell the two apart.
A program set for the file's extension comes first, as for Enter.
.TP
.B Alt+O
Open with: the programs the entry under the cursor can be opened with, to
pick one with Enter or 1 to 9.
Those set for its extension come first; then, on Linux and the BSDs, the
applications the desktop says take its type, from the same
.I mimeapps.list
and
.I mimeinfo.cache
files
.BR xdg\-open (1)
reads; then the system's default, and on Windows its own Open With dialog.
One marked
.B [terminal]
runs in this terminal.
E edits the
.I open
file.
Inside an archive the file is copied out first.
.TP
.B Backspace
Go to the parent directory.
.TP
.B Home End
First or last entry.
.TP
.B PageUp PageDown
A page up or down.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Left Ctrl+Right
Open the directory under the cursor in the left or right panel.
.TP
.B Ctrl+U
Swap the panels, each with its tabs, cursor, selection and history;
the focus goes with the one it was in, and neither is read again.
.TP
.B Alt+I
Send the other panel to the active one's directory.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Edit the active panel's path in place, in the line above it, and go there
with Enter; Esc, or a click anywhere else, leaves it as it was.
A click on either panel's path does the same, in that panel.
.B Tab
fills out a name as far as the entries agree;
.B ~
is the home directory, and a path not starting with
.B /
is taken from the panel.
A file is landed on, in the directory it is in.
.TP
.B Alt+Left Alt+Right
Back and forward through the directories the tab has been in, landing on
the entry the cursor was on.
Each tab keeps its own.
.TP
.B Alt+Down
Every directory either panel has been in this run, across all their tabs,
newest first, opening on the newest that is not the panel's own.
Typing narrows it to the paths holding every word typed; Up and Down
choose, Enter goes, Esc closes.
.TP
.B Ctrl+D
Bookmarks.
.B Enter
or
.BR 1 ... 9
goes to one,
.B A
or
.B Insert
keeps the panel's directory,
.B D
or
.B Delete
forgets the one highlighted, and
.B Shift+Up Shift+Down
move it in the list.
.TP
.I letters and digits
Quick search: jump to the first name starting so, either case; Up and Down
go between the names that do.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the search; close a dialog, the viewer or the editor; stop counting
directory sizes; stop waiting on a directory that has not answered.
.TP
.B Space Insert
Select or deselect the entry.
Space on a directory also counts its size, unless it is a link to one.
.TP
.B Alt+Shift+Enter Alt+Enter
Count the size of every directory in the panel, a few at a time, the rest
shown waiting.
Links to directories are left out.
As
.BR du (1)
counts: a file with several hard links once, and nothing on
.IR /proc ,
.I /sys
and the like.
It also turns on the space map: a bar behind each row, as long against the
largest entry as the entry is, as
.BR ncdu (1)
draws it.
The cursor's row keeps its colour; the lines under the panels give its share
of the whole.
The map stays with the directory, gone while the panel is elsewhere.
.TP
.B + \-
Select or deselect by pattern:
.B *
any run of characters,
.B ?
any one,
.B [abc]
and
.B [!a\-z]
classes, several separated by
.BR ; .
A pattern ending in
.B /
is for directories.
All in lower case matches either case.
.TP
.B *
Invert the selection of files.
.TP
.B Alt+*
Invert the selection of files and directories.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S
Filter the panel: only what the pattern matches is shown, narrowing as it
is typed.
A word without \fB*\fR, \fB?\fR or \fB[\fR is looked for anywhere in the
name; a pattern takes directories as well as files, and one ending in
.B /
directories only.
Enter keeps it, shown in the path bar; Esc puts back the filter there was;
an empty one shows everything.
What is selected but filtered out is let go.
The filter stays while the panel is in its directory, and goes when it
moves elsewhere.
.TP
.B Ctrl+B
Flat view: every file under the panel's directory in one list, each by its
path from there; sorted by size, it finds what takes the space.
Directories are walked, not listed, and links to them are not followed.
Operations work on the files as anywhere, and F2 renames one where it is.
Patterns, for the filter and for + and -, go by each file's own name; one
with a
.B /
inside it, and a plain word in the filter, by the path.
Inside an archive too.
At most 100000 files are listed.
Ctrl+B again goes back; going anywhere else drops it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Undo the last rename, copy, move or move to the trash, and again the one
before, up to 32 jobs back: a copy is deleted, a move or a rename put back,
what went to the trash restored from it (not on macOS, which says so).
A step whose target has changed since is refused and asked about.
What wrote over a file, or merged into a directory, is not kept to undo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+E
The panel's names in the editor, a numbered line each: edit a line to rename,
delete it to delete, duplicate it to copy.
Saving shows the plan; Enter runs it as one job, Esc goes back to the lines.
A line that cannot be had says which, and nothing runs.
.TP
.B Alt+B Alt+Shift+B
Alt+B puts what is chosen in the basket, from any directory; Alt+Shift+B shows
the basket in the panel, all of it selected, for F5, F6 or Alt+P at once, and
again goes back.
Alt+B in the basket takes entries out of it, not off the disk.
.TP
.B Alt+D
Find the files under the directory, or among what is selected, that are the
same as another: by size, then by SHA\-256.
The groups are fed to the panel with all but one of each selected.
Empty files, links and second names of one file are left out.
.TP
.B Alt+R
A dossier of the directory: the kinds of file that take its room, the
largest and the newest, its code's languages by lines, git's commits over
the last eight weeks, and the copies in it.
.TP
.B Alt+G
Git on what is chosen:
.B S
stages it,
.B U
unstages it,
.B D
throws away the changes that are not staged, after asking, and
.B C
shows the file beside HEAD's.
.B R
lists the branches, tags, stashes and last commits, narrowed as typed, and
opens the one chosen - or a revision typed, as HEAD~3 - as a read\-only
folder, as an archive is gone into, at
.IR <top>/@git:<revision> .
.TP
.B Alt+H
Work out the checksums of what is chosen, a directory's files all the way
down, as a job: SHA-256, SHA-512 and MD5 at once, in one reading of each
file.
Tab or Right shows the next algorithm's, Shift+Tab or Left the one before.
C copies them \- one file's checksum alone \- and Enter writes them as
.BR sha256sum (1)
does, to
.I name.sha256
for one file or
.I SHA256SUMS
for several.
On a checksum file, written by
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR md5sum (1)
or in BSD's style, check the files it names instead, the failures listed
first.
.TP
.B Alt+A
Change the permissions, owner, group and modified time of what is selected,
or of the entry under the cursor.
A field left as it opened, or emptied, changes nothing.
The mode is octal, such as
.BR 755 ,
or as
.BR chmod (1)
takes it, such as
.BR u+x,go\-w ;
owner and group by name or number; the time as
.BR "2026\-10\-23 14:02:11" .
Tab, or a click on a field, moves between them.
Only the time on Windows.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C Alt+C
Copy the full paths, or the names, of what is selected, or of the entry
under the cursor, to the clipboard, a line each; on
.BR .. ,
the panel's own directory.
Sent over OSC 52, which the terminal has to allow.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F3 Ctrl+F4 Ctrl+F5 Ctrl+F6
Sort both panels by name, extension, date or size;
the same key again reverses the order.
A click on a column heading does the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Reload both panels.
.TP
.B Ctrl+T Ctrl+W
New tab, close tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+PgUp Ctrl+PgDn
Previous or next tab.
.TP
.B Alt+1 ... Alt+9
Go to tab 1 to 8, or the last.
.TP
.B Alt+F1 Alt+F2
Choose a drive for the left or right panel.
Ctrl works too.
.SS Function keys
The ones the screen answers are listed along the bottom border;
a click on one presses it.
.TP 18
.B F1
Help: the keys, the FM84 logo in the theme's colours, then
.BR About ,
with the version, licence and where fm84 lives.
Like every popup it sits over the panels and is no taller than they are;
when it is longer, the arrows, PageUp and PageDown, Home and End and the
mouse wheel scroll it.
.TP
.B F2
Rename.
With a selection, rename every entry selected by a pattern:
.B [N]
the name,
.B [E]
the extension,
.B [C]
a counter;
.B [N2\-5]
letters 2 to 5,
.B [N3\-]
from the third on,
.B [C10+5:3]
counting from 10 by fives in three digits;
.B [Exif:date]
when a photograph was taken, with
.BR [Exif:day] ,
.BR [Exif:time] ,
.B [Exif:make]
and
.BR [Exif:model] ;
.BR [Tag:artist] ,
.BR [Tag:title] ,
.BR [Tag:album] ,
.B [Tag:track]
and
.B [Tag:year]
from a song's tags; and
.B [Pdf:title]
and
.B [Pdf:author]
from a PDF.
A file that does not say what is asked is marked, not renamed.
Find and Replace come after the pattern, and a change of case last.
A table shows each old name beside its new one, marking those that cannot
be had \- given twice, taken by something else, or not a plain name \- and
nothing is renamed until none is.
Names that go where another is leaving are moved through names of their own
first, so none is written over.
Where a drive takes two names for one that the table told apart \- a FAT
stick on Linux taking ax and Ax for the same \- the second is refused and
named, and the first stays.
.TP
.B Shift+F2
Compare the panels' directories, as in Total Commander, and select in each
what differs from the other: an entry only it has, and of a file both have,
the newer, or both when the times agree and the sizes do not.
Times within two seconds count as the same, for FAT.
Directories both have are not gone into.
What was selected before is replaced, and the status bar says how many
differ.
.TP
.B F3
View the file.
.TP
.B = Shift+F3
Compare two files side by side: two selected in the active panel, or else
the file under the cursor against the one of the same name in the other
panel, or failing that the one under the cursor there.
Between the two,
.B |
marks a changed line,
.B <
one only on the left and
.B >
one only on the right.
F3 or N goes to the next difference, Shift+F3 or P to the previous; Esc
closes it.
Files that are the same, differ only in line endings, or are not text are
reported in the status bar instead.
Shift+F3 needs a terminal with kitty's keyboard protocol, such as kitty, foot,
WezTerm, Ghostty or Alacritty; elsewhere it cannot be told from a report of
the cursor's position, and
.B =
is the key.
The same holds for Ctrl+F3 and for Shift+F3 in the viewer.
.TP
.B F4
Edit the file, in the built\-in editor or the one set under F11.
.TP
.B F5
Copy to the other panel, or wherever the dialog is told.
A single file can be given a new name on the way.
Before anything is written, what goes to each filesystem is set against
what can still be written there, and a copy without the room asks first.
With
.B Verify copies
on, each file is read back from where it went and checked against the
original before it takes its place.
.TP
.B Alt+F5 Ctrl+Shift+F5
Make a symbolic link to what is chosen, asking where as F5 does: for one
entry, its name in the other panel; for several, the other panel's
directory, with a link in it for each.
Each points at the absolute path of what it links to, and nothing already
there is written over.
Tab makes hard links instead: files only, on the same filesystem, and of a
symbolic link the file it points at.
Ctrl+Shift+F5 is as in Total Commander; kitty keeps it for reloading its
config, so Alt+F5 is there too.
.TP
.B F6
Move, as F5 copies.
.TP
.B F7
Create a directory.
.TP
.B Shift+F4
Create an empty file.
.TP
.B F8 Delete
Move to the trash, or delete if F11 says so.
.TP
.B Shift+F8 Shift+Delete
Delete for good.
.TP
.B F9
Open a terminal in the panel's directory.
.TP
.B Alt+M
The user menu: commands of your own from the
.I menu
file, run in the shell in the panel's directory with the terminal handed
over, until Enter is pressed after.
In a command,
.B {file}
is the entry under the cursor,
.B {name}
its name,
.B {files}
what is chosen,
.B {dir}
the panel's directory and
.B {other}
the other panel's.
They reach the shell as variables, so any name arrives whole; leave them
unquoted.
Enter or 1 to 9 runs one, and E edits the menu.
.TP
.B Alt+F9 Ctrl+F9
Extract the whole of the archive under the cursor, or of the one the panel is
in, to the other panel or wherever the dialog is told.
.TP
.B Alt+P
Pack what is chosen into a new archive, asking where: on the other panel's
directory, named after what is packed.
Tab goes through the formats by the ending of the name.
.TP
.B F10
Quit.
Twice while a copy, move or delete runs; asks first about unsaved edits.
.TP
.B Shift+F10
Quit, leaving
.B cd84
in the directory it started from.
.TP
.B F11
Options.
.TP
.B F12
Preview the file under the cursor in the other panel.
.TP
.B Alt+F7 Ctrl+F7
Find files by name and, if asked, by content, size and age, down from the
active panel's directory.
Containing is text, or with the switch under the fields a regular
expression, in which
.B ^
and
.B $
are each line's ends.
Size is
.BR >10M ,
.BR <1K ,
.BR >= ,
.B <=
or
.BR 1M..10M ,
in K, M, G and T of 1024.
Modified is an age, as
.B <7d
newer than a week,
.B >1y
older than a year,
.B 1d..7d
between, in min, h, d, w, mo and y; or a date:
.B 2026\-10\-01
that day,
.B >2026\-10\-01
after it,
.B 2026\-10\-01..2026\-10\-31
from one to the other.
Tab goes through the fields, Space turns the switch.
On the results, Enter goes to the one chosen and
.B F
feeds them all to the active panel, as one list under the directory the
search started in, each by its path from there.
.TP
.B Alt+S
Synchronize the panels' directories: compare them all the way down and show
a plan of what differs.
What one side lacks is copied to it, and the newer of two files over the
older; two of an age but not of a size, and a file meeting a directory, are
left.
In the plan, Right and Left copy that way, Delete or D deletes from the side
that has it alone, Space or = leaves it,
.B >
and
.B <
make one side as the other is, deleting what only it has, and R puts the plan
back.
Enter runs it as one job, writing over what is in the way; a plan that
deletes asks for a second Enter, unless Confirm delete is off.
Hidden entries are left out unless the panels show them, links are never
followed, and times two seconds apart are the same.
Not inside an archive, nor with one directory inside the other.
.SS During a copy, move or delete
.TP 18
.B Esc
Cancel it.
.TP
.B R S A
Retry, Skip, or Skip all, when an entry fails.
.TP
.B R C
Check again, or go on anyway, when the target has not the room for what
is about to be written - asked before anything is.
Esc stops, the reason left in the status bar.
.TP
.B Y N K A S B O
Overwrite, Skip, Keep both, Overwrite all, Skip all, Keep both all, or
Overwrite all older, when a file is already there.
Keep both leaves it, and writes the new one beside it as
.IR "name (2).ext" ,
numbered on past any already taken.
.TP
.B D
Delete for good, when the trash will not take an entry.
.SS Viewer
.TP 18
.B X
Hex view on or off; for an image, cycle Image, Text and Hex.
.TP
.B W
Wrap long lines, or scroll them sideways.
.TP
.B F
Fit or fill an image; on text, follow the file as it grows, as
.BR "tail \-f" ;
scrolling up stops it.
.TP
.B + \-
Zoom an image in or out.
.TP
.B PageUp PageDown
Previous or next picture in the folder, when one is open.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Copy the selection.
.SS Editor
.TP 18
.B Shift+arrows
Select text.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Select all.
.TP
.B Ctrl+X Ctrl+C Ctrl+V
Cut, copy, paste.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Undo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Y Ctrl+Shift+Z
Redo.
.TP
.B Tab Shift+Tab
Indent the selected lines a step, or bring them, or the cursor's line, a step
back: a tab in a file indented with tabs, the tab width of spaces otherwise.
Tab with nothing selected types a tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+/ Ctrl+_ Ctrl+7
Make the selected lines, or the cursor's, comments with the file's own mark,
at their shallowest indent; or, when they all are, code again.
.TP
.B Enter
Break the line, the new one as indented as the one it came from.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R F4
Replace; then
.BR Y ,
.B N
or
.B A
to replace, skip or replace all.
By a regular expression,
.BR $1 ,
.B ${name}
and
.B $0
in the replacement are filled in from each match.
.TP
.B O R
Overwrite or reload, when the file changed on disk since it was opened.
.SS Viewer and editor
.TP 18
.B Ctrl+F
Find.
All in lower case matches either case.
.TP
.B Alt+R
In the Find and Replace prompts, look for a regular expression instead of
text, or for text again.
.TP
.B F3 Shift+F3
Next or previous match.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G
Go to line.
.PP
Scroll bars are drawn in the frame's border, down the right and along the
bottom, when there is more than fits.
A click on the track scrolls a page that way; the thumb can be dragged.
In the editor they move the view and leave the cursor where it is.
.SH OPTIONS DIALOG
F11 opens the options, changed in place with Enter, Space, Left or Right and
saved as they change.
Among them: hidden files, sort order, columns, git marks, date format and size units;
confirmations, delete to trash, what to do when a destination exists,
verifying copies, the terminal and editor commands, remembering directories; the theme, icons,
clock, and the cursor's shape and whether it blinks; tab width, line numbers,
what the scroll bars are drawn with, highlighting and image settings.
.PP
.B Info column, from commands
adds a column filled by the commands in the
.I columns
file, by extension; E on its row edits them.
.BR "Outrun progress bar" ,
.B Burst what is deleted for good
and
.B Turbo
are for fun, all off to start with: a job drawn as a car driving towards the
sun, the rows of a delete for good bursting first, and the neon turned up
with scanlines over the panels.
.B Language
is Automatic, which follows the environment (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
or one of English, Bulgarian, Czech, Danish, German, Greek, Spanish, French,
Irish, Croatian, Italian, Hungarian, Macedonian, Dutch, Norwegian
Bokm\(oal, Polish, Brazilian Portuguese, Romanian, Slovak, Slovenian, Serbian
in the Cyrillic alphabet, Serbian in the Latin alphabet, Swedish and Turkish,
in that order - by each one's own name; the keys stay the same in any of
them.
The list scrolls within the panels when it does not fit, with Up and Down or
the mouse wheel.
.PP
.B Verify copies
reads each file a copy, a move to another disk or a synchronize writes back
from the device, flushed and let go of from memory first, and compares its
SHA\-256 with the original's before it takes its place.
One that reads back differently is thrown away and asked about as any
failure is.
Slower: for USB sticks and network mounts.
.PP
The cursor, in fields and in the editor, is the terminal's own: a bar, a
block or an underline.
.B Classic
draws a reversed cell instead, for a terminal that cannot change the shape of
its cursor.
.PP
In the
.B Terminal
and
.B Editor
commands,
.B {}
stands for the directory or the file.
An editor without
.B {}
gets the file last.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/config
The options, as plain
.I key = value
lines, fine to edit by hand.
.I ~/.config/fm84/config
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set, and
.I %APPDATA%\efm84\econfig
on Windows.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/session
The panels' directories and tabs, kept when
.B Remember directories
is on.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/bookmarks
The directories kept with
.BR Ctrl+D ,
a
.I bookmark = path
line each, in order.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/menu
The commands of
.BR Alt+M ,
a
.I label = command
line each, in order.
Made with examples the first time the menu opens.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/open
The programs files are opened with, by extension, a line each as
.IR "pdf epub = zathura" ;
.B *
is every file.
The first line that fits is what Enter and Shift+Enter use.
The command runs in the shell in the file's directory, with
.B {file}
where the file goes, or on the end; a leading \fB!\fR runs it in this terminal.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/columns
The commands that fill the Info column, by extension, a line each as
.IR "jpg png = identify \-format '%wx%h' {file}" ;
the first line a command prints is what the column shows.
Made with examples the first time F11 edits it.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/scores
F1's high scores, counted as the jobs finish.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config directory is; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B HOME
The config directory when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set, and where a typed
.B ~
leads.
.TP
.B TERMINAL
The terminal F9 opens when none is set under F11.
Without either, fm84 tries
.BR xdg\-terminal\-emulator ,
.BR alacritty ,
.BR kitty ,
.BR foot ,
.BR gnome\-terminal ,
.BR konsole ,
.B xfce4\-terminal
and
.BR xterm ,
in that order.
.TP
.B LANGUAGE LC_ALL LC_MESSAGES LANG
The language fm84 is shown in, while F11's Language is Automatic: the first
of LANGUAGE's colon\-separated list that fm84 has words for, unless the
locale is C; otherwise the first of the other three that is set.
A locale is taken by its language:
.BR bg ,
.BR cs ,
.BR da ,
.BR de ,
.BR el ,
.BR es ,
.BR fr ,
.BR ga ,
.BR hr ,
.BR it ,
.BR hu ,
.BR mk ,
.BR nl ,
.B nb
or
.BR no ,
.BR pl ,
.BR pt ,
.BR ro ,
.BR sk ,
.BR sl ,
.BR sr ,
.B sv
and
.BR tr ,
as
.BR de_AT.UTF\-8 ;
anything else is English.
Serbian is in the Latin alphabet unless the locale asks for Cyrillic, as
.B sr@cyrillic
or
.B sr\-Cyrl
does.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the trash is, as the freedesktop.org trash specification has it.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_DIRS XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
With XDG_DATA_HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME, where Alt+O looks for the
applications that take a file's type and the defaults chosen among them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Quit normally.
.TP
.B 1
Not run from a terminal, or an error that stopped it, writing the
.B \-\-choosedir
file among them.
.TP
.B 2
A mistake on the command line, such as an unknown option, a directory that
does not exist or a shell
.B \-\-shell\-init
has no
.B cd84
for.
.SH NOTES
Icons need a Nerd Font; F11 can switch them to plain letters.
Images are drawn as ASCII art in colour, and look best in a terminal with
true colour.
